# Fuzzing
#
# The seed corpus under fuzz/corpus is checked in - those are the inputs somebody thought worth
# writing down, and fuzz/smoke.sh runs over them. What libFuzzer *finds* is not: it names new
# inputs after their SHA-1, and a few seconds of smoke produces hundreds of them. smoke.sh keeps
# them out by writing to a scratch corpus under the build directory, but a target run by hand can
# still be pointed straight at fuzz/corpus, so the forty-hex-digit names are ignored outright.
fuzz/corpus/*/[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]

# A build directory that is not called cmake-build-* (the sanitizer build in CI, say).
fuzz-corpus/
